# Clean Code — Python & SQL

## Overview

| Principle | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| Readability | Code is read more than written |
| Simplicity | Prefer clear steps over clever one-liners |
| Consistency | Same patterns for ingest, transform, and SQL layers |
| Testability | Pure functions, explicit inputs/outputs, SQL you can `EXPLAIN` |

---

## Naming conventions

### Python — descriptive names

```python
# ❌ Bad — unclear names
d = datetime.now(timezone.utc)
u = fetch_user(1)

def calc(a, b):
    return a * b

# ✅ Good — intent-revealing
run_timestamp_utc = datetime.now(timezone.utc)
active_customer = fetch_user(customer_id)

def calculate_line_total(unit_price: Decimal, quantity: int) -> Decimal:
    return unit_price * quantity
```

### Python — consistent patterns

```python
# Booleans: is_, has_, should_, can_
is_active = True
has_admin_role = user.role == "admin"
should_refresh_cache = cache_age_seconds > 300
can_write = has_admin_role and is_active

# Functions: verb + object
def fetch_orders_since(since: datetime) -> list[Order]: ...
def transform_bronze_to_silver(df: DataFrame) -> DataFrame: ...
def validate_schema(rows: Iterable[dict]) -> None: ...

# Pipeline stages (common in DE)
def extract_from_api(endpoint: str) -> list[dict]: ...
def load_to_bronze(records: list[dict], run_id: str) -> None: ...
```

### SQL — identifiers and aliases

```sql
-- ❌ Bad — cryptic aliases and vague columns
SELECT o.id, u.n, SUM(l.p * l.q)
FROM o JOIN u ON o.uid = u.id JOIN l ON l.oid = o.id
GROUP BY o.id, u.n;

-- ✅ Good — readable aliases; names match domain language
SELECT
    orders.order_id,
    customers.email,
    SUM(order_lines.unit_price * order_lines.quantity) AS line_revenue
FROM orders
INNER JOIN customers ON orders.customer_id = customers.customer_id
INNER JOIN order_lines ON order_lines.order_id = orders.order_id
GROUP BY orders.order_id, customers.email;
```

