## SQL structure

### Prefer CTEs for readable stages (warehouse / analytics SQL)

```sql
-- ❌ Bad — nested subqueries that hide the grain
SELECT *
FROM (
    SELECT customer_id, SUM(amount) AS s
    FROM (
        SELECT * FROM sales WHERE sale_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'
    ) x
    GROUP BY customer_id
) y
WHERE y.s > 1000;

-- ✅ Good — named steps; grain is obvious
WITH daily_sales AS (
    SELECT *
    FROM sales
    WHERE sale_date >= DATE '2024-01-01'
),
customer_totals AS (
    SELECT
        customer_id,
        SUM(amount) AS total_amount
    FROM daily_sales
    GROUP BY customer_id
)
SELECT *
FROM customer_totals
WHERE total_amount > 1000;
```

### Filter early; avoid `SELECT *` in production transforms

```sql
-- ❌ Bad — drags unused columns through joins
SELECT *
FROM fact_orders
INNER JOIN dim_customer ON fact_orders.customer_sk = dim_customer.customer_sk;

-- ✅ Good — explicit columns for the layer you are building
SELECT
    fact_orders.order_id,
    fact_orders.order_date,
    dim_customer.country_code
FROM fact_orders
INNER JOIN dim_customer ON fact_orders.customer_sk = dim_customer.customer_sk
WHERE fact_orders.order_date >= :start_date;
```

### One conceptual job per CTE (mirrors small functions in Python)

```sql
-- Each CTE = one transformation or filter step; easier to test mentally and to comment
WITH source_filtered AS (
    SELECT * FROM bronze_events WHERE event_date = CAST(:run_date AS DATE)
),
deduplicated AS (
    SELECT * FROM (
        SELECT
            *,
            ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY event_id ORDER BY ingested_at DESC) AS rn
        FROM source_filtered
    ) t
    WHERE rn = 1
)
SELECT * FROM deduplicated;
```

